🔎 Step-by-step reading of the diagram

STEP A — WRITE side (left → cluster)
  Transaction Service (Producer) builds a payment/transfer event.
  It calls send(event, key=userId).
  Kafka uses the key to pick ONE partition (same userId → same partition).
  Result: producer writes the event ONCE into the cluster. Done for write path.

STEP B — Inside KAFKA CLUSTER (storage)
  Topic `transactions` is logical only (a name).
  Real storage = Partition 0 / 1 / 2 (ordered append-only logs).
  Each message in a partition has an offset: [0][1][2][3]...
  Partitions are spread across Broker1 / Broker2 / Broker3.
  For each partition: leader accepts writes; followers replicate for safety.

STEP C — READ side starts (cluster → consumer groups)
  After data is stored, many services can READ it independently.
  Kafka does NOT push one message to only one app forever.
  Instead: each Consumer Group reads its own copy of the stream (fan-out).

STEP D — Same group = load sharing (look inside one box)
  Example: Group fraud-service
    C1 → Partition 0
    C2 → Partition 1
    C3 → Partition 2
  Meaning: 3 consumers process in parallel.
  One message in P0 is handled by C1 only (inside this group).
  Rule: 1 partition → at most 1 active consumer in the SAME group.

STEP E — Different groups = fan-out (look across 3 boxes)
  fraud-service, notify-service, ledger-service each have their own groupId.
  So the SAME transaction event is processed by ALL three groups.
  That is why one write can power Fraud + Notifications + Ledger together.

STEP F — Offsets (why each group says “own offsets”)
  Each group tracks “how far I have read” per partition separately.
  fraud-service can be at offset 100 while notify-service is still at 80.
  If a service crashes / restarts, it resumes from its last committed offset.
  You can also rewind offsets → replay old events (within retention).

STEP G — Final business action (bottom of diagram)
  After consuming:
    fraud-service      → Fraud Detection
    notify-service     → send SMS / push / email
    ledger-service     → update balances / ledger entries
  Kafka’s job ends at delivery of events; business logic lives in each service.


How to remember in 4 lines (interview short version):
1) Producer writes once into topic partitions (on brokers).
2) Same group  → partitions are shared (parallel load).
3) Different groups → each group gets a FULL copy of the stream (fan-out).
4) Offsets are tracked per group + per partition (replay possible).


📦 Example of Kafka Topic Structure

Topic: order-events

Partition 0: [offset 0] [offset 1] [offset 2] ...
Partition 1: [offset 0] [offset 1] ...

Each consumer group keeps its own offsets per partition,
so multiple apps can process the same data independently.

Mini walk-through:
