💡 Best Practices
Choose the right partitioning key (balance + ordering needs).
Monitor consumer lag to detect slow consumers.
Use dead letter topics for failed messages.
Enable retry and error handling in consumers.
Use idempotent producers / idempotent consumers to handle duplicates.
Use acks=all + replication for critical financial data.
Keep consumers in separate groups when every service must see every event.


🎯 Quick Interview Answers Cheatsheet
Q: Why Kafka over RabbitMQ?
A: Need durable event log, replay, very high throughput, many independent consumers (stream/event platform).

Q: How does Kafka scale?
A: Add partitions + consumers (and brokers). Parallelism is driven by partitions.

Q: Is order guaranteed?
A: Only within a partition. Use a key to keep related events ordered.

Q: What happens if a broker dies?
A: Partition leaders fail over to follower replicas (if replication is configured).
